{"meta":{"course":"cat100-supplement","chapter":"06","section":"02","chapter_title":"GitHub Pages Personal Website","title":"Starter HTML and CSS","description":"CAT 100 textbook section on starter HTML and CSS, centered on an accessibility revision pass, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","learning_objectives":[{"id":"cat100-supplement-06-02-lo-01","statement":"Construct a minimal valid HTML document, placing metadata (title, charset, stylesheet link) in <head> and visible content in <body>"},{"id":"cat100-supplement-06-02-lo-02","statement":"Write descriptive alt text for informative images and justify empty alt for decorative ones against WCAG 2.2 SC 1.1.1"},{"id":"cat100-supplement-06-02-lo-03","statement":"Predict which CSS rule wins when selectors conflict, using specificity first and source order as the tiebreaker"},{"id":"cat100-supplement-06-02-lo-04","statement":"Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions"}],"concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_github_pages_personal_website","cat100_supplement_starter_html_and_css","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":2,"top_tier_hardened":false,"prereq_section_ids":["cat100-supplement/06/01"],"practice_ids":["cat100_supplement_6_2_starter_html_and_css_artifact_signal","cat100_supplement_6_2_starter_html_and_css_annotation_signal","cat100_supplement_6_2_starter_html_and_css_ai_boundary","cat100_supplement_6_2_starter_html_and_css_calibration","cat100-supplement_06_02_lo_04_direct_check"],"lo_practice_map":{"cat100-supplement-06-02-lo-01":["cat100_supplement_6_2_starter_html_and_css_artifact_signal"],"cat100-supplement-06-02-lo-02":["cat100_supplement_6_2_starter_html_and_css_calibration"],"cat100-supplement-06-02-lo-03":["cat100_supplement_6_2_starter_html_and_css_annotation_signal","cat100_supplement_6_2_starter_html_and_css_ai_boundary"],"cat100-supplement-06-02-lo-04":["cat100-supplement_06_02_lo_04_direct_check"]},"alignment_method":"deterministic lexical mapping v2 with direct-evidence fallback; instructor review required"},"content":"# Starter HTML and CSS\n\n<figure-block caption=\"Organizational reference model connecting Starter Html And Css, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning to an evidence-based revision decision.\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_github_pages_personal_website\" purpose=\"organizational\" source=\"ALGET original instructional diagram\" license=\"Project-authored; repository license\">\n  <img src=\"/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat100-supplement/06-02-starter-html-and-css.png\" alt=\"Three connected concept cards for Starter HTML and CSS: Starter Html And Css, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning, followed by an evidence-to-revision transfer prompt.\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" />\n</figure-block>\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nHTML and CSS are two different languages with two different jobs, and most beginner errors come from blurring them. HTML (HyperText Markup Language) describes structure and meaning: this is a heading, this is a paragraph, this is an image. CSS (Cascading Style Sheets) describes appearance: this heading is dark blue, the body is centered at 640 pixels wide. Mixing the jobs (using a heading tag just to make text big, for example) produces pages that look acceptable but read poorly to screen readers and search engines.\n\nThis section teaches you to write a minimal, valid HTML document and one stylesheet that controls its look, with two specific skills that beginners most often get wrong: writing accessible image markup and predicting which CSS rule wins when two rules conflict. These are the foundation for everything you publish in the rest of the module.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Construct a minimal valid HTML document, placing metadata (title, charset, stylesheet link) in <head> and visible content in <body>.\n- Write descriptive alt text for informative images and justify empty alt for decorative ones against WCAG 2.2 SC 1.1.1.\n- Predict which CSS rule wins when selectors conflict, using specificity first and source order as the tiebreaker.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA student writes his first About page, opens it in a browser, and it looks fine: a big title, a photo, a short bio. He submits it. The reviewer runs a screen reader and hears: \"About Me, heading. Image. First-year data student.\" The photo announced only the word \"image\" because the markup had no alt text, so a blind visitor learns nothing about it. Later the student adds a second CSS rule for the heading color and is surprised when the heading turns red instead of the navy he wanted; he did not know the later rule overrode the earlier one.\n\nBoth problems are invisible when you only judge a page by how it looks on your own screen. This section makes them visible by teaching you to read markup and the cascade, not just preview the result.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nThree mechanics carry most of the weight in starter HTML and CSS.\n\n**The minimal valid document.** Every HTML page needs a small, fixed skeleton: a `<!DOCTYPE html>` line, an `<html>` element with a `lang` attribute, a `<head>` for metadata the visitor does not see (the `<title>`, the character set, the stylesheet link), and a `<body>` for the content they do see. The `<title>` lives in the head and controls the browser tab and the bookmark name; it is not visible on the page itself. Getting this skeleton right is what makes a file a web page rather than a text file.\n\n**Accessible images.** The `alt` attribute on an `<img>` is the text a screen reader announces and the text a browser shows when an image fails to load. `alt` should describe the image's content or function, not repeat the word \"image.\" A decorative flourish can use empty `alt=\"\"` so screen readers skip it; a meaningful photo needs a real description. [WCAG 2.2](https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/) treats missing alt text on informative images as a failure of Success Criterion 1.1.1 (Non-text Content), a Level A criterion, which is why this is not optional polish.\n\n**The cascade and specificity.** \"Cascading\" in CSS means rules can conflict, and the browser resolves conflicts with predictable rules. When two rules target the same element with the same specificity, the one written later wins. When specificity differs, the more specific selector wins regardless of order (an id beats a class, a class beats a bare tag). Understanding this is what separates \"I changed a color and the whole page broke\" from \"I know exactly which rule is in charge.\"\n\nThese mechanics connect to the course anchors. [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students) makes the alt-text requirement an equity issue: a page without it excludes blind visitors. [NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined) reframes clean, valid markup as a professional habit; sloppy HTML is the kind of thing a technical reviewer notices immediately.\n\n<inline-check question=\"An informative headshot is marked up as <img src='me.jpg' alt='image'>. What does the accessibility check say?\" explanation=\"alt='image' just repeats the word the screen reader would already imply and describes nothing. For an informative image, alt must describe content or function, for example alt='Maria Lopez, headshot'. Only a purely decorative flourish may use empty alt='' so the screen reader skips it.\" options='[{\"text\":\"It fails: alt must describe the content or function, e.g. alt=\\\"Maria Lopez, headshot\\\"\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"It passes: any non-empty alt attribute satisfies [WCAG](https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/)\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"It should be alt=\\\"\\\" because all images are decorative\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Worked Example\n\nYou are building the starter page for your About section. Follow the markup from a broken first pass to a working, accessible version.\n\n**Step 1. Lay out the minimum valid document.**\n\n```html\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n  <head>\n    <meta charset=\"utf-8\">\n    <title>Maria Lopez | Portfolio</title>\n    <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"assets/style.css\">\n  </head>\n  <body>\n    <h1>About Me</h1>\n    <img src=\"headshot.jpg\">\n    <p>First-year data student at CAT 100.</p>\n  </body>\n</html>\n```\n\n**Step 2. Find the two problems.** The browser tab reads correctly because `<title>` sits in `<head>`, but the image fails accessibility: `<img src=\"headshot.jpg\">` has no alt text, so a screen reader announces only \"image\".\n\n**Step 3. Fix the image and add one CSS rule.**\n\n```html\n<img src=\"headshot.jpg\" alt=\"Maria Lopez, headshot\">\n```\n\n```css\n/* assets/style.css */\nbody { font-family: system-ui, sans-serif; max-width: 640px; margin: 2rem auto; }\nh1 { color: #1f3a5f; }\n```\n\n**Step 4. Confirm the cascade.** If a later rule said h1 { color: #b00; } it would win over #1f3a5f at equal specificity, so keep only the rule you intend.\n\nThe page now sets the correct tab title, gives the image a real alt description, and applies a stylesheet whose single h1 rule is unambiguous. Every change is visible in the markup, not just in how the page looks.\n\nPredict the cascade before you reveal it. Two rules target the same heading, so the resolution is deterministic, not random.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Predict the heading color when two equal-specificity rules conflict\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"Your stylesheet has h1 { color: #1f3a5f; } near the top and h1 { color: #b00; } pasted at the bottom. Both are bare-tag selectors (equal specificity). What color renders?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Navy #1f3a5f, because it was written first\",\"Red #b00, because the later rule wins at equal specificity\",\"Undefined, the browser picks at random\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"At equal specificity the rule written LATER in source order wins, so the heading is red #b00. Source order is the tiebreaker, never randomness.\"},{\"prompt\":\"What is the correct fix to get the navy you intended?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Add !important to the navy rule\",\"Delete the stray red rule so the source says exactly one thing\",\"Move the navy rule to the bottom and hope\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Delete the stray red rule. Reaching for !important is a blunt tool that creates worse conflicts later; the clean fix is to make the source state one thing about the heading color.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Specificity overrides order: an id beats a class beats a bare tag regardless of where it sits. Order only decides ties. Knowing both rules is the difference between debugging the cascade and fighting it.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nBefore you call an HTML page finished, read the markup, not the preview, against three checks:\n\n1. **Structure check:** Does every visible piece of content sit in a tag that matches its meaning (heading for headings, paragraph for prose, list for lists)? Never reach for a heading tag just to make text bigger; that is what CSS font-size is for.\n2. **Accessibility check:** Does every informative `<img>` have descriptive `alt` text? Read each alt aloud and ask whether a visitor who cannot see the image learns what they need to.\n3. **Cascade check:** For any element you styled, can you name which rule is winning and why? If two rules target it, the later one wins at equal specificity; if you did not intend that, delete or rename one.\n\nIf you cannot pass all three by reading the source, the page may look done but is not done.\n\n**You do not have to catch every issue by eye.** Run your HTML through the [W3C Markup Validation Service](https://validator.w3.org/) for structure, and a browser accessibility audit (Lighthouse in Chrome/Edge DevTools, or axe DevTools) to flag images with missing alt text. Treat their reports as evidence in your revision trace.\n\nOrder the parts of a minimal valid HTML document from the outside in. Some pieces live in the head (unseen metadata) and some in the body (visible content), and the skeleton only validates when they nest in the right sequence.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"<!DOCTYPE html> declaration on the first line\",\"<html lang=\\\"en\\\"> opening element\",\"<head> holding the charset, <title>, and stylesheet link\",\"<body> holding the visible h1, img with alt, and paragraphs\",\"Close </body> then </html>\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nA frequent misreading is believing that \"it looks right in my browser\" means the markup is correct. Browsers are forgiving: they will render a page with a missing closing tag or no alt text without complaint. The errors surface only for screen-reader users, on slow connections, or when you add the next rule and the cascade behaves unexpectedly.\n\nA second misreading is treating CSS specificity as random. It is fully deterministic. When a color refuses to change, the cause is almost always a more specific selector elsewhere winning the conflict, not a mysterious bug.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nApply the checks to a real fix. A student's page shows a headshot, but the alt attribute reads `alt=\"image1\"`, the filename from her phone. The accessibility check fails: \"image1\" tells a screen-reader user nothing. She rewrites it as `alt=\"Maria Lopez, headshot\"`, which names the person and the type of image. That is a content decision, not a style decision, so it belongs in the HTML.\n\nNext she wants the page title navy. She adds `h1 { color: #1f3a5f; }`. Later, copying a snippet from a tutorial, she pastes `h1 { color: #b00; }` at the bottom of the same stylesheet. The heading turns red. Running the cascade check, she sees two rules of equal specificity targeting `h1`; the later red rule wins. She does not fight it with `!important` (a blunt tool that creates worse conflicts later); she simply deletes the stray rule so only the intended navy remains. The fix is to make the source say exactly one thing about the heading color.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nA strong reader can predict what a browser will do before opening it. As you study the worked example, cover the result and ask: given this markup, what will a screen reader announce for the image? Given these two CSS rules, what color will the heading be? If your predictions match the browser, you are reading the code rather than guessing from the preview.\n\nA strong reader of starter HTML and CSS can answer four questions about any page:\n\n1. Which content belongs in the head (unseen metadata) and which in the body (visible content)?\n2. Does each image's alt text describe its content or function, or does it just repeat the word image?\n3. When two style rules conflict, which one wins, and is that the one you intended?\n4. If you removed the stylesheet entirely, would the bare HTML still be readable and correctly structured?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nYour page is strong when the markup alone (with no CSS) is still a clean, ordered document: one main heading, real paragraphs, images with honest alt text, links that say where they go. CSS should improve a page that is already correct, never rescue a page that is structurally wrong. A reviewer who reads your source should be able to understand the page's meaning without seeing it rendered.\n\nA strong submission notes one cascade decision you made on purpose: \"I kept the navy rule and deleted the red one I had pasted, because two h1 color rules conflicted and only the intended one should remain.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nTake the same skills to a different document type: a single landing page for a campus club instead of a personal About page. The HTML skeleton is identical (doctype, lang, head, body), and the cascade rules are unchanged. What shifts is the content and therefore the markup choices: the club page may need a list of meeting times (a real `<ul>`, not paragraphs with dashes) and a logo whose alt text states the club name. The principle that structure should match meaning and that conflicts resolve by specificity and order does not change; only the specific tags you reach for do. Being able to carry the rules to new content is the sign you learned the language and not just one page.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the starter HTML and CSS accessibility pass packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat100-supplement/06-02-starter-html-and-css-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"starter HTML and CSS accessibility pass\" course=\"CAT 100\" section=\"06.02\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted. When a coding assistant such as GitHub Copilot (in VS Code) or ChatGPT/Claude hands you CSS, check that its selectors match elements that actually exist in your markup before you paste it in; the exact tool and model version change often, but the verification habit does not.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the starter HTML and CSS accessibility pass.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the starter HTML and CSS accessibility pass for CAT 100 06.02. A peer comment points to an interface move issue connected to [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What would make this section more useful while you revise?\" options='[\"It asks you to name a decision, point to evidence, and choose support that helps the revision.\", \"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"0\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_starter_html_and_css\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, check that the starter HTML and CSS accessibility pass records audience, evidence source, AI/software support, accepted revision, rejected suggestion, and confidence change.\n\n## Concept Map\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat100_supplement_starter_html_and_css\", \"cat100_supplement_github_pages_personal_website\", \"evidence_based_revision\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## References\n\n- [NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)\n- [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students)\n- [WCAG accessibility](https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/)\n- [W3C Markup Validation Service](https://validator.w3.org/)\n- [MDN Web Docs: HTML element reference](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Element)\n\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat100_supplement_6_2_starter_html_and_css_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_starter_html_and_css","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_starter_html_and_css","cat100_supplement_github_pages_personal_website"],"stem":"You want the page title that shows in the browser tab to read 'Maria Lopez | Portfolio'. Which markup is correct?","options":["<h1>Maria Lopez | Portfolio</h1> placed inside <body>","<title>Maria Lopez | Portfolio</title> placed inside <head>","<header>Maria Lopez | Portfolio</header> placed inside <html>","<p class=\"title\">Maria Lopez | Portfolio</p> placed anywhere"],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The browser tab text comes from the <title> element inside <head>. <h1> shows on the page itself; it does not set the tab.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_6_2_h1_sets_tab_title","difficulty":"easy","learning_objective_ids":["cat100-supplement-06-02-lo-01"]},{"id":"cat100_supplement_6_2_starter_html_and_css_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_starter_html_and_css","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_starter_html_and_css"],"stem":"Your CSS file has two rules in this order: 'p { color: blue; }' then 'p { color: green; }'. A paragraph with no other styles renders what color?","options":["Blue, because it was defined first","Green, because for equal specificity the later rule wins","Black, because the two rules cancel out","Red, the browser default for conflicts"],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"When two selectors have the same specificity, the rule that appears later in the stylesheet overrides the earlier one, so the text is green.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_6_2_first_rule_wins","difficulty":"medium","learning_objective_ids":["cat100-supplement-06-02-lo-03"]},{"id":"cat100_supplement_6_2_starter_html_and_css_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_ai_supported_learning","concept_ids":["ai_supported_learning","cat100_supplement_starter_html_and_css"],"stem":"A coding assistant (such as GitHub Copilot in VS Code, or ChatGPT/Claude) gives you CSS that styles your nav bar but targets a class .container that does not exist in your HTML. What should you do before pasting it in?","options":["Paste it as-is; the AI already tested it","Check which selectors match elements that actually exist in my HTML, then add or remove the .container class so the markup and CSS agree","Delete all my existing CSS so there are no conflicts","Keep the unused .container rule because more CSS is safer"],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"AI-generated CSS often references classes that are not in your HTML. You stay responsible for making selectors and markup match before the styles will do anything.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_6_2_ai_css_runs_blindly","difficulty":"hard","learning_objective_ids":["cat100-supplement-06-02-lo-03"]},{"id":"cat100_supplement_6_2_starter_html_and_css_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_starter_html_and_css","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_starter_html_and_css","evidence_based_revision"],"stem":"You write <img src=\"headshot.jpg\"> for your About page. A reviewer using a screen reader cannot tell what the image is. What is the correct fix?","options":["Make the image larger with width=\"600\"","Add a descriptive alt attribute: <img src=\"headshot.jpg\" alt=\"Maria Lopez, headshot\">","Convert the image to PNG","Wrap the image in a <b> tag"],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A screen reader announces the alt text. Adding alt=\"Maria Lopez, headshot\" makes the image meaningful to non-visual users; size and format do not address accessibility.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_6_2_alt_text_optional","difficulty":"medium","learning_objective_ids":["cat100-supplement-06-02-lo-02"]},{"id":"cat100-supplement_06_02_lo_04_direct_check","type":"conceptual","stem":"Provide a concise response that demonstrates this learning objective: Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.","expected_answer":"The response accurately demonstrates the section objective: Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions, using relevant section concepts, evidence, or calculations.","explanation":"This direct-evidence item is used when no closed-response item adequately samples the objective. Score it with the section concepts and worked examples as the rubric.","difficulty":"medium","learning_objective_ids":["cat100-supplement-06-02-lo-04"]}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"cat100_supplement_6_2_h1_sets_tab_title","pattern":"confusing_h1_with_title","description":"Believing the page heading <h1> controls the browser tab text rather than the <title> element in <head>","trigger":"learner edits <h1> expecting the browser tab to change","feedback":"The tab text comes from <title> inside <head>. <h1> is the visible heading on the page. They are two different elements with two different jobs.","rail_action":"explain"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_6_2_first_rule_wins","pattern":"first_css_rule_wins","description":"Assuming the first CSS rule always wins when in fact, at equal specificity, the later rule in source order overrides the earlier one","trigger":"learner predicts the earlier color for two same-specificity rules","feedback":"At equal specificity the cascade takes the last matching rule. Reorder the rules or raise specificity if you need a different one to win.","rail_action":"represent"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_6_2_ai_css_runs_blindly","pattern":"ai_css_assumed_correct","description":"Pasting AI-generated CSS without checking that its selectors match classes and elements that actually exist in the HTML","trigger":"learner applies AI CSS that targets a non-existent class and is surprised nothing changes","feedback":"A selector only styles elements it matches. Confirm every class in the CSS exists in your HTML, then keep, add, or drop classes so the two agree.","rail_action":"ask"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_6_2_alt_text_optional","pattern":"alt_text_treated_as_optional","description":"Treating the alt attribute on images as decorative or optional rather than as the accessible description a screen reader announces","trigger":"learner ships an <img> with no alt or an empty alt for a meaningful image","feedback":"Give meaningful images a short, specific alt describing what they show. That is how non-visual users receive the same information sighted users get.","rail_action":"practice"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"e4f72d7197f475d048f280e032c5227e00bfd2dc8397fde02992ce54a49bf518","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}